# Appendix H — Testing and verification reference

This appendix maps claims to the repository checks that can support them. The
maintenance method is developed in
[Developing the book and program together](developing_book_and_program.html),
self-consistency in
[A generator that generates itself](generator_that_generates_itself.html), and
conservative failure policy in
[Resource bounds and fail-closed behavior](resource_bounds_fail_closed.html).

No single command proves the whole system. Compilation, unit tests, generated-file
comparison, fixed-point verification, package inspection, real-host acceptance, and
human publication review answer different questions.

## Test ownership: generated is not ordinary source

Most files under `src/test/` are ordinary authoritative source and should be edited
directly. Six test or acceptance files are exceptions: they are generated outputs
owned by the literate book.

The generated test roots are:

- `src/test/headless/publication-headings.test.ts`
- `src/test/headless/publication-settings.test.ts`
- `src/test/headless/publication-highlight.test.ts`
- `src/test/headless/publication-fonts.test.ts`
- `src/test/headless/publication-screen.test.ts`
- `src/test/acceptance/screen-host.js`

Their root assembly is recorded in
[Appendix A — Program assembly maps](assembly.html), and their constituent fragments
live with the publication policies they verify. Edit those `.literate` chapters and
regenerate; a direct edit to one of these six files will be overwritten.

All other test files are ordinary source unless and until a Literate output root says
otherwise. This includes the remaining headless tests, `src/test/headless/vscode-fixture.ts`,
`src/test/headless/authoritative-book.ts`, the remaining `src/test/acceptance/`
harnesses and evidence descriptions, and the VS Code test runner under
`src/test/suite/`. Package metadata and test scripts are ordinary source as well.

Generated tests are still real tests: they compile, run, fail, and require independent
expectations. Their ownership merely keeps a policy's explanation, implementation,
and selected regression cases in one authoritative chapter. Ordinary tests remain
appropriate when a fixture or cross-cutting harness is clearer outside the book.

## Suite organization

### Headless regression tests

TypeScript headless tests live under `src/test/headless/` and compile to
`out/test/headless/`. They use Node's test runner and cover the engine, parser, CLI,
transactions, editor adapters with a VS Code fixture, fragment symbols, browser
presentation, publication, self-hosting, projections, direct language-server
protocol, diagnostics, navigation, and code actions.

`src/test/headless/vscode-fixture.ts` supplies the mocked editor surface used by
adapter tests. `src/test/headless/authoritative-book.ts` loads `index.literate`, all
chapter sources, and the source template, then exposes fresh generation results to
checks that compare projections or generated artifacts against the actual book.

Compile the TypeScript test tree without running it:

```sh
yarn test-compile
```

Run a focused compiled group after that command, for example:

```sh
node --test out/test/headless/language-*.test.js \
  out/test/headless/extension-adapter.test.js \
  out/test/headless/editor-symbols.test.js \
  out/test/headless/code-actions.test.js
```

A focused command is useful while iterating, but it does not include lint,
generated-file comparison, unrelated headless suites, packaging, or real hosts.

### Complete default suite

The default repository suite is:

```sh
yarn test
```

Its script builds the publication Mermaid worker, compiles the project, lints
TypeScript, runs every compiled `out/test/headless/*.test.js`, and finally runs the
CLI generated-file check. It therefore detects stale generated source or HTML, but
it does not perform self-host fixed-point verification, package a VSIX, run real
editor UI, invoke every publication acceptance harness, or judge rendered pages.

### VS Code extension-host suite

Run the extension test entry with:

```sh
yarn test:extension
```

This builds the TypeScript and bundled extension, then invokes the VS Code test
runner. The host runner may download its configured VS Code test host and requires a
suitable graphical environment. Its sample/integration coverage supplements rather
than replaces the headless suite and interactive smoke testing.

### Publication acceptance scripts

The package scripts expose focused real-tool routes:

```sh
yarn test:publication
yarn test:pdf
yarn test:polish
yarn test:annotations
yarn test:assets
yarn test:mermaid
yarn test:browser-security
```

The first six build the CLI and invoke the corresponding Python acceptance harness.
They cover portable layout, PDF compilation, typography probes, annotations, local
assets, and Mermaid publication. `test:browser-security` compiles the project, builds
the worker, and runs the browser security harness. These routes may require installed
external tools, fonts, browser, TeX resources, or explicit harness arguments; they do
not install those prerequisites.

Additional opt-in scripts under `src/test/acceptance/` exercise real editor hosts,
configured language servers, automatic completion UI, publication commands, screen
presentation, and packaged runtime isolation. Many intentionally consume existing
builds and download nothing. Read each harness header or adjacent acceptance document
before running it: executable paths, display needs, platform constraints, timeouts,
and evidence destinations differ by boundary.

## Generated-file verification

Use either command to compare the complete current generation plan with disk without
writing outputs:

```sh
yarn literate check
yarn check:generated
```

A valid check compares every planned source and HTML destination as `current`,
`missing`, or `stale`. It does not detect or delete orphaned files left behind by a
removed or renamed root. Inventory review is required for that case.

For a read-only command invocation that must not rebuild the CLI wrapper first, use a
known existing executable:

```sh
node out/cli-entry.js check
```

The direct command reads saved disk inputs. It does not see unsaved editor buffers.
Do not run CLI generation concurrently with editor generation against the same
project.

## Fixed-point verification

The fixed-point route is:

```sh
yarn check:self
```

Equivalently, `yarn literate self-check` names the underlying CLI command. It first
requires all planned outputs to match disk. It then:

1. generates a fresh complete plan from the captured book;
2. overlays fresh generated TypeScript in memory over ordinary checkout sources;
3. type-checks that combined program without replacing project files;
4. bundles the fresh engine and runs it in a bounded child process;
5. requires the child to return a well-formed plan without diagnostics; and
6. compares destination sets and exact content, independent of array order.

If `S` is the captured snapshot, `P` is its fresh plan, and `E` is the engine built
from that plan, the verified condition is:

```text
E(S) = P
```

This proves that fresh generated sources type-check in context and that the fresh
engine reproduces the complete plan from which it was built. It does not prove CLI
reporting, transaction recovery, editor behavior, publication quality, external-tool
compatibility, or safety of the project code it executes. Run it only for trusted
self-hosting source.

## Package verification

A build-directory test does not establish archive contents. After:

```sh
yarn package:vsix
```

inspect the actual `out/<name>-<version>.vsix`. The opt-in
`src/test/acceptance/publication-package.mjs` harness can consume existing runtime
bundles or an explicit existing VSIX. It checks the runtime allowlist, byte identity
between archive and reviewed bundles, dependency-graph closure, publication-only
request boundaries, snapshot publication, portable resources, and isolated helper
execution. It does not build, generate, install, or download its prerequisites.

Package closure is not a complete transitive license audit. Review
`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, manifest declarations, and portable resource licenses
separately.

## Security regression catalogue

The security posture is distributed across focused suites rather than one test named
“security.” Use this catalogue to find the relevant boundary.

### Parsing, graph, and output paths

`engine.test.ts`, `parsing.test.ts`, `inspection.test.ts`, and CLI tests cover invalid
fragment declarations, missing references, cycles, ordering, portable paths,
template handling, complete-plan rejection, and structured diagnostics. Transaction
suites cover symlink and special-file rejection, duplicate and ancestor collisions,
exclusive staging, rollback, cleanup failures, retained recovery paths, permission
behavior, and concurrent-writer limitations. See
[Changing files safely](changing_files_safely.html).

### Editor and source mutation

Editor adapter, symbol, code-action, input-watching, and publication-extension tests
cover trust, workspace authority, buffer precedence, stale snapshots, provider
registration, command confinement, prompt cancellation, destination containment,
serialized writers, and commit-aware cancellation. Mutating projected-source tests
require complete, non-overlapping edit sets to map into one current unique fragment
occurrence; unsafe partial edits reject the whole action. See
[Mutating projected source safely](mutating_projected_source.html).

### Language-server protocol and projection

Language client, projection, feature, navigation, diagnostics, and action suites cover
JSON-RPC framing, UTF-8 byte lengths, UTF-16 coordinates, envelope validation,
capability gating, bounded synchronization, cancellation, stale replies, unsafe URIs,
wrapper and seam omission, repeated expansions, diagnostic freshness, untrusted
Markdown, command rejection, and whole-action atomicity. Real-server harnesses add
representative compatibility evidence but do not imply support for every server. See
[Appendix E — Language-server protocol details](appendix_language_server_protocol.html).

### Browser programs

Mermaid preview, publication exploration, and browser security tests cover inert JSON,
script-sensitive escaping, payload shape, ownership that cannot be forged through DOM
attributes, panel preflight, cycles, depth and byte budgets, link authorization,
Mermaid directives and active-content rejection, network denial, SVG validation,
browser sandbox requirements, stale render suppression, process cleanup, and bounded
logs and artifacts. See [Appendix D — Browser programs](appendix_browser_programs.html).

### Publication formats and resources

Pandoc, annotation, LaTeX, rail, font, asset, Mermaid, PDF, runtime, helper, and CLI
publication suites cover raw author-content rejection, stable identities, annotation
ambiguity, heading links, geometry, TeX escaping, manifest schemas, license presence,
binary signatures, path aliases, hard links, companion collisions, private stages,
process groups, convergence, output bounds, request schemas, runtime separation, and
preservation of a previous publication on failure. See
[Appendix F — Publication formats and protocols](appendix_publication_protocols.html).

Security tests establish enforced policy at named boundaries. They do not turn TeX,
project-authored self-hosting code, a browser, or a language server into a sandbox.

## Verification routes by change

Use these as starting points, then add the affected subsystem's focused tests.

### Prose-only chapter change

```sh
node out/cli-entry.js check
```

After deliberate regeneration, require no unexpected generated program-source diff.
Review generated HTML, links, headings, diagrams, and publication order where the
prose move changes structure.

### Fragment implementation change

```sh
yarn literate generate
yarn test
yarn esbuild
```

Review generated source before testing. Add a focused test near the changed invariant.
If the engine or generator changed, also run `yarn check:self`.

### CLI or transaction change

Run the focused CLI and transaction tests, then `yarn test`. Exercise both human and
`--json` output when the response contract changes. Use fault-injection cases for
preflight, rollback, recovery, and committed cleanup.

### Editor integration change

Run the focused headless adapter tests, `yarn test`, and `yarn esbuild`. Add extension
host or interactive evidence when behavior depends on real workspace trust, commands,
preview, completion widgets, editor buffers, filesystem providers, or cancellation UI.

### Language-service change

Compile, run the focused `language-*.test.js` group, then the full suite. Use the
appropriate installed-server or UI harness for claims about real protocol
compatibility or visible editor behavior. Confirm that requests do not materialize
generated files.

### Publication change

Run the focused headless publication suites and the corresponding acceptance script.
A format or renderer change may require real Pandoc, browser, font, or TeX evidence.
Inspect warnings and pages; successful PDF compilation is not a typography review.

### Packaging or release change

Build all shipped entry points, run the full suite and relevant fixed-point checks,
create a VSIX, inspect its entries, and run package or installed-host acceptance for
changed surfaces. Test the exact archive intended for publication.

## Supported environments and known limits

- Headless tests require no graphical editor, but some focused publication tests use
  injected adapters and therefore do not prove real tool compatibility.
- The extension-host route needs a suitable VS Code host and display and may acquire
  its configured test host.
- Direct language-server acceptance requires explicitly installed server executables;
  it neither installs servers nor proves universal LSP compatibility.
- Real publication acceptance requires the external tools appropriate to the tested
  route. Mermaid needs an explicit browser; PDF needs the supported TeX process model.
- Editor-driven process ownership is restricted to trusted local file workspaces;
  remote, web, and virtual environments retain only the capabilities their adapters
  can support safely.
- Filesystem tests establish recoverable exception behavior, not crash durability,
  simultaneous multi-file visibility, or protection from external concurrent writers.
- Deterministic engine plans do not imply byte-identical PDF or diagram output across
  external toolchains and fonts.
- Resource bounds fail closed at their defined boundaries but do not prove a universal
  CPU, memory, disk, or filesystem-latency limit.

Report validation in terms of the command actually run and the claim it supports.
“The headless suite passed,” “the fixed point passed,” “the VSIX was inspected,” and
“the PDF was reviewed” are complementary statements, not interchangeable ones.
